	abstract = {We study the temperature dependence of the conductivity due to quantum interference processes for a two-dimensional disordered itinerant electron system close to a ferromagnetic quantum critical point. Near the quantum critical point, the cross-over between diffusive and ballistic regimes of quantum interference effects occurs at a temperature \$ T{\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}ast\}=1/{\textbackslash}tau {\textbackslash}gamma (E\_\{F\}{\textbackslash}tau){\textasciicircum}\{2\}\$, where \${\textbackslash}gamma \$ is the parameter associated with the Landau damping of the spin fluctuations, \${\textbackslash}tau \$ is the impurity scattering time, and \$E\_\{F\}\$ is the Fermi energy. For a generic choice of parameters, \$T{\textasciicircum}\{{\textbackslash}ast\}\$ is smaller than the nominal crossover scale \$1/{\textbackslash}tau \$. In the ballistic quantum critical regime, the conductivity behaves as \$T{\textasciicircum}\{1/3\}\$.},
